00:01:50.924 --> 00:01:55.284
those positions would be managed
by clearing house rather than

00:01:55.284 --> 00:02:00.084
causing immediate contagion into
the rest of the financial system.

00:02:00.084 --> 00:02:05.535
[MUSIC]

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:09.548
The banks are much better capitalized
now than they were before the crisis,

00:02:09.548 --> 00:02:10.560
dramatically so.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:17.901
And yet when they get credit
from wholesale bond investors,

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:24.090
the bond investors are asking for
much higher compensation

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:29.178
in yield for
bearing the risk of default losses.

00:02:29.178 --> 00:02:30.950
Now, how do you square those two facts?

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.121
First the banks are much safer,
much less likely to fail.

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:40.733
And secondly, bond investors
are requiring much more compensation for

00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:45.260
bearing losses associated
with bank failure.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:50.205
There's only one reasonable
explanation in my mind,

00:02:50.205 --> 00:02:54.935
and that's the perception
by investors that we're

00:02:54.935 --> 00:02:59.355
a large US bank to get into
trouble in the future.

00:02:59.355 --> 00:03:01.340
The government is not
going to bail them out.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.872
The government is not going to make those
bonds whole, the difference between

00:03:06.872 --> 00:03:11.516
the likelihood that big banks got bailed
out before the financial crisis versus

00:03:11.516 --> 00:03:16.180
the likelihood that they would get bailed
out in the future there's dramatic.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.573
It's a significant reduction and

00:03:18.573 --> 00:03:23.368
the probability of bailout that's
implied by those market prices.

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:28.939
[MUSIC]

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:31.840
Imagine a very large bank on death's door.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.028
Or maybe it's just getting
under-capitalized and

00:03:35.028 --> 00:03:38.950
the government has lost patience
with the managers of the bank.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:47.729
And has become concerned that the bank may
fail and cause problems for the economy.

00:03:47.729 --> 00:03:55.852
Now, regulators have on their dashboard
a big red button called bail in.

00:03:55.852 --> 00:04:01.419
If they hit that button then
the bank will go through a failure

00:04:01.419 --> 00:04:06.877
resolution process by which
overnight the debts of the bank

00:04:06.877 --> 00:04:12.350
are eliminated,
a large fraction of them are eliminated.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.403
And the creditors that were owed those
debts are no longer creditors to

00:04:18.403 --> 00:04:21.755
the bank they are now
shareholders in the bank.

00:04:21.755 --> 00:04:23.015
And they therefore,

00:04:23.015 --> 00:04:28.110
will suffer the losses that the taxpayers
might have otherwise suffered.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.626
That process is called bail in.

00:04:30.626 --> 00:04:33.554
And of course there are it's
a complicated process and

00:04:33.554 --> 00:04:37.067
is being implemented in different
ways in different countries.

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:41.180
Within the United States it's taken
quite seriously by bond investors.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:46.331
That's one of the reasons that they
no longer believe in too big to fail,

00:04:46.331 --> 00:04:49.119
is that they know that that red button,

00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:54.624
is sitting on the desk of the regulators
waiting to be hit when the time comes.

00:04:54.624 --> 00:04:57.433
And that the plans for
this failure resolution,

00:04:57.433 --> 00:04:59.977
have been fairly methodically worked out.

00:04:59.977 --> 00:05:05.510
